    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d754">

                <pb facs="1026" n="1014" sameAs="frus:frus1945v06/pg1014"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 P.W./12–2945</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The War Department to the
                            Department of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">OPD 336 TS</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-12-29">29 December,
                        1945</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#flush">Attention: Japan—Korea Economics Division</p>
                <p rendition="#flush">Subject: Request for Information on Cyclotrons in Japan</p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>Receipt is acknowledged of your letter of 10 December 1945<anchor n="73"
                            xml:id="d754-f1"/> enclosing a copy of dispatch No. 517/45 dated 27
                        November 1945 from <pb facs="1027" n="1015" xml:id="pg1015"/> the Minister
                        of Australia<anchor n="74" xml:id="d754-f2"/> concerning the reported
                        destruction of cyclotrons in Japan by order of the Supreme Commander for the
                        Allied Powers.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>In reply to your request for information of the facts of the destruction
                        and the justification therefore, the following extract from the press
                        conference of the Secretary of War of 14 December 1945 is quoted: <quote
                            rend="blockquote">
                            <p>“<hi rendition="#small-caps">Press</hi>: Mr. Secretary, if no more
                                questions on this, I have one on the destruction of the cyclotrons
                                in Japan. MacArthur said your office ordered that.</p>
                            <p>
                                <hi rendition="#small-caps">Mr. Patterson</hi>: That is quite
                                correct. I personally never saw the message but there was nothing
                                strange about that because hundreds of messages go out of here from
                                the Secretary of War that do not come to my personal notice. In this
                                particular case, I believe that the matter was not handled with the
                                thorough consideration that the matter warranted and that the
                                sending of that particular message was a mistake, but in the theater
                                to General MacArthur it was a directive and he took the proper
                                action based upon the message. I am not certain what the answer
                                would have been if the matter had received the thorough
                                consideration that it should have had.</p>
                            <p>
                                <hi rendition="#small-caps">Press</hi>: Is anyone being disciplined
                                over it, Mr. Secretary?</p>
                            <p>
                                <hi rendition="#small-caps">Mr. Patterson</hi>: No, I can make
                                argument both ways on that question. You can see it was a case of
                                mistake in the War Department.”</p>
                        </quote>
                    </item>
                    <label>3.</label>
                    <item>The following is suggested as a possible reply to the Minister of
                            Australia:<anchor n="75" xml:id="d754-f3"/>
                        <quote rend="blockquote">
                            <p>“General MacArthur was directed to destroy the Japanese cyclotrons in
                                a radio message sent in the name of the Secretary of War. That
                                message was dispatched without having been seen personally by the
                                Secretary and without its having been given the thorough
                                consideration which the subject deserved.</p>
                            <p>“While the officer who originated it felt that the action directed
                                was in accordance with the War Department’s established policy of
                                destroying Japan’s war potential, the dispatch of such a message
                                without first investigating the matter fully was a mistake, which is
                                regretted by the War Department.”</p>
                        </quote>
                    </item>
                </list>
                <closer>For the Secretary of War: <lb/>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">R. L. Vittrup</hi>
                    </signed>
                    <lb/>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Colonel, GSC</hi>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
